Not on Legend of Zelda. So Legend of Zelda is actually getting a special version later in the road, which I'm really excited for because they are specifically calling them out as a special version.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:32.680
Let's make a pause.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:36.440
Yeah. Hold on. It wouldn't delete them.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:43.360
Okay. Legend of Zelda is specifically making a special version.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.420
I think they also said there was going to be a special version of Super Mario Bros.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:47.640
3 and Metroid.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.460
So I'm excited to see what these revised NES games might be like.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:07:04.600
But right now, only the games that actually had two-player in them support this online two-player where, like, you know, me and you can play and you can be in your apartment and I can be at my apartment and we can play Balloon Fight or Mario Bros.

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:06.080
You know? Like, that's cool.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:07.220
No, that is awesome.

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.520
I am – not that I'm holding out, but I do wish that at some point in the future they make that tweak.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.100
That's my favorite thing to do on the PlayStation 4.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:16.220
Oh, yes. Passing the controller.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:17.360
Passing the controller back and forth.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:21.420
I'd actually be really interested to see if the – you know, if you want me to hypothesize for a minute.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:35.320
I think that these special editions of these games could be kind of like a co-op feature where there's two links now and you can be doing stuff in Hyrule and the player two can be doing other things in Hyrule.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:39.880
Similar to, like, maybe Metroid, it would be two Samuses and you kind of just, like, link up whenever you want.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:41.740
Like, I think that would be really cool.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:45.280
That would be really cool. I can't imagine what else would constitute a special edition.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.260
Like, loot boxes.

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:49.360
Loot boxes, yes.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:50.880
Exactly. And new skins. Yeah.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:51.900
No longer –

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.900
No longer does the old man in the cave offer you the sword.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.780
You have to pay 99 cents to unlock the sword.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.140
All right, guys. Enjoy the episode of Gradius.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:12.160
Hopefully, if you don't like the Nintendo Switch and you don't own one and this was boring, hopefully you hit the chapter marker that I put in there for you because iOS 12 also now supports podcast chapters, so your native podcast app can do chapters as well.

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:13.700
Don't plug iOS 12.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:19.420
Gradius, it's a duel to the death with the evil Bacterions.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:41.380
And welcome to NEStalgia, chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:43.000
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:46.660
And I'm Joe. Guys, today we're talking about Gradius, I hope.

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.320
I'm really not sure if that's how you say it.

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:50.320
I think it's Gradius.

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:52.460
I always thought Gradius as well.

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:59.280
There's something weird about, like, going online and asking how made-up words are – like, how do they sound?

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:04.700
You know? It's kind of like – Joe, you'll relate to this – with Roxas, and I used to call him Roaxas.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:08.200
Well, Mike, you don't know how to pronounce many words.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:10.120
But it's also verbally spoken in the game.

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:11.380
It's not like you read it.

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:15.760
Okay, yeah, yeah. That actually sounds really stupid, so I need to rewind even further.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:17.400
I have some of those with, like, Harry Potter and stuff.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:23.860
I'm talking about, like, yeah, like, before we ever knew – like, before the game had ever came out, I was on Kingdom Hearts Insider.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.220
Right. And this is not a Kingdom Hearts episode, so forget about it.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:30.400
We're talking about Gradius or Gradius, but we're going to leave it at Gradius for the rest of the game.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.800
You did promise last week that you would know how to say it.

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:35.640
Yeah, I know. And then I looked up online, and there was, like, multiple opinions.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:40.260
But it's the first horizontal shmup that we have for the NES.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:45.460
That's true. It's a lot – you know, it's a lot different than 1942 in that regard, if you think about it.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:47.500
I mean, forget, like, just the setting and stuff like that.

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:53.820
But, you know, playing horizontal, I feel like it's a little more claustrophobic than playing vertical.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:58.700
Yeah, I think that also had something to do with, like, just the size of your ship and everything else.

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:02.940
And that's the design. I think they just had more, like, variety in enemies, so there was more on the screen at once.

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:06.000
Right. But if we can jump right into first impressions.

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:12.200
I'd love to hear it. I would just like to say that this is the first game since Super Mario Bros.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:17.100
that I felt, like, actually came out on, like, an advanced console.

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:30.380
Okay. Just because it was fast, it was fluid, it looked great, it sounded great, and I didn't feel like all of the enemies were just going to flicker out of existence at any point.

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:33.540
This is just a very well-performing, good-looking game.

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:37.060
Yeah, I think, like, you know, I'll jump in with my first impressions here.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:43.660
I know the arcade version, and this is definitely, like, a step down, but it's not that 1942 level step down.

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:52.980
Oh, yeah. This is a pretty competent port for Gradius, and you can kind of tell, like, nothing looks so different than it did in the arcade version that you're like, oh, well, I don't really know what that is.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:54.760
I mean, like, it looks pretty good, in my opinion.

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:59.400
I think it oozes, like, a lot of personality, a lot of creative design, you know.

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:04.740
Each level is almost like its own theme and its own world kind of, you know, even more so.

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:05.580
So it is. Yeah.

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:07.860
More so than Super—well, not its own world.

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:10.320
It actually all takes place on the same planet, if you can believe that.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:12.880
Even more so than, like, Super Mario Bros., you know.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:18.440
It's like in Super Mario Bros., you had, like, World 1, World 2, and stuff like that, but they didn't look, like, so different, you know.

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:22.280
In this, it's like I really felt like I was in a different area each time.

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:28.540
Yeah, you're either going to, like, a magma area or an Easter Island head asteroid belt.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:31.080
Like, I don't know what that was about, but I dig it.

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:37.120
Right. And then you got—at the end, you got that Super Fortress, or whatever they call it.

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:42.340
I think it's got a name, a really strange name, if we have a man—you mean the boss battle there?

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:44.140
Yeah, the final stage.

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:50.520
Yeah. I mean, I know that that's, like, you're basically, like, coming up against the Bacterian army at the end there, but, like, Joe and E—

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:58.580
Yeah, so I think we're all kind of on the same page with—my first thing I thought was, like, this is one of the most polished games that we've seen so far, I think.

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:05.260
In terms of, like, the visuals and the colors and even the music, because there's, like, a lot of music changes in this.

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:08.960
Yeah. Which is a lot to ask for, it seems like, in an NES game.

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:10.880
So it doesn't get old that way.

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:14.240
Yeah, it felt very cleaned up and very polished.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:23.800
Yeah, and I think, like, just to go back to my first impression real fast, because I have played the arcade version, you know, the game is still just as hard as any other shmup, too.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:25.640
Like, don't get me wrong, I died a ton.

00:12:25.640 --> 00:12:32.560
And, you know, sometimes if the game is good enough, like we were talking about in Ghosts and Goblins, like, that's satisfying.

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:43.040
Yeah. And I think going away from difficulty for one moment and just going into your—the differences between the arcade and the NES version,

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:56.800
I think they're only, in terms of performance, was that you could only have, like, two of those little followy guys instead of four, and just, like, a graphical downgrade.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:59.260
I think that it's— You're talking about option there, right?

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:00.740
Yeah, option, yes.

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:04.900
I think that other than that, it's pretty much, like, frame by frame.

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:07.800
Yeah, so just a little housekeeping for a second.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:17.180
This is Konami's first game for the NES, and, you know, no longer do we associate Konami with, you know, good graces.

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:19.520
They pretty much just like to make Pachinko.

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:20.880
Yeah, it's Pachinko.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:24.460
Yeah, Pachinko games and Metal Gear Online.

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:26.760
Pachinko. Yeah, Pachinko.

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:37.720
So, you know, it's kind of interesting to see, like, even their very first release on the NES is a pretty decent game that was pretty well-respected in the arcade, and then the port turns out to be good, too.

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:46.060
Yeah. I'm going to say that botched this name here, but Hiroyasu Mataguchi, he is the director for this game.

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:56.020
This game is a sequel to Scramble, another horizontal shmup that pretty much, like, invented that genre a little bit, and they wanted to make a Scramble 2.

00:13:56.400 --> 00:14:02.560
And he was kind of, like, trying to figure out, well, how do you do something different than what Scramble did?

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:17.060
And essentially, like, what it comes down to is that power-up system of being able to, like, collect power-ups, choose your power-ups, and kind of, like, sacrifice, like, okay, I can get this power-up right now, or kill a couple more enemies, and I'll have, like, a much better power-up.

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:27.120
Yeah, this has a really good feeling of progression in terms of grabbing those power cores and choosing what to activate at what time.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:35.960
Do I want to get the missiles now, or do I want to keep saving up until I can have that force field?

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:39.220
I think that adds a layer of depth to it.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:52.500
Right, and just to paint a picture, you have, like, this menu bar at the bottom, and every time you grab, I don't know if it's every time you grab a power-up, but after a certain amount of power-ups, the menu, the selector automatically moves everyone.

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:53.800
It moves everyone every single time.

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:54.960
Every single time.

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:56.100
Oh, it's every single time.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:03.040
So you have speed-up, missile, double, laser, option, and question mark, and also loops.

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:04.240
Yeah, it moves back, too.

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:11.780
So if you get another power-up, you're back to speed-up, and if that's not what you wanted, which was the first one, then you should have used your power-up when you wanted it.

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:17.080
And theoretically, each power-up is better than the one before it, but I didn't find that necessarily true.

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:20.760
I really think that, like, option is, like, a mandatory selection.

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:25.900
Like, you want that stuff early on, and you want it, like, you know, you want it for as long as you can hold on to it.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:32.460
I think that and just the force field actually gives you the ability to withstand fire.

00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:37.400
And I think especially in a shmup game, that is very important.

00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:49.000
Right. So the player controls the trans-dimensional spaceship Vic Viper, and, you know, you have to destroy, like, waves of enemies through various environments in order to,

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:55.040
and this is now, like, a famous saying in the Gradius and Shmup universe, is just destroy the core.

00:15:56.340 --> 00:15:58.520
It's like a thing people shout at each other.

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:03.380
Yeah, yeah, yeah, like, it's a huge thing, because if you didn't notice, at the end of every level, you have to destroy a core.

00:16:03.820 --> 00:16:06.140
Right. Well, I mean, I didn't consider that a core.

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:07.800
I considered that, like, a Millennium Falcon.

00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:10.540
Yeah, okay. That's actually really funny that you bring that up.

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:12.380
It did look like something familiar.

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.880
A Millennium Falcon is a good way of describing it.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:19.800
Yeah, I think the only reason I knew that those were cores was because that's how they were described in the manual.

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:26.860
Right, right. But in the arcade, it's referenced, and it's also just, like, a reoccurring thing in the Gradius series.

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:29.580
Okay. And Gradius is a series, for those of you that don't know.

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:32.080
It's actually a pretty popular series, all things considered.

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:35.940
