for me. That's like the Moai Island stage or the later like amoebas and stuff like that.

00:58:46.380 --> 00:58:50.060
But I think like for the most part, the game is like.

00:58:50.980 --> 00:58:56.120
It's not unfair, like it's not like, you know, nothing in this game to me screams like

00:58:56.120 --> 00:58:57.340
Red Devil. Yeah.

00:58:57.660 --> 00:58:59.280
That kind of level of frustration.

00:58:59.280 --> 00:59:05.160
I would say that your style of play is or the game allows your style of play to be more

00:59:05.160 --> 00:59:12.600
expressive so that you can go in with different weapons and like whether or not you have the

00:59:12.600 --> 00:59:16.360
shield or you can go in with different loadouts and try your luck a different way.

00:59:16.380 --> 00:59:22.420
And you have you have a better experimentation process than you would with Ghosts and Goblins,

00:59:22.520 --> 00:59:23.340
I think. Absolutely.

00:59:23.520 --> 00:59:28.060
Yeah. With especially just because it's different between like space exploration and platforming

00:59:28.060 --> 00:59:32.920
is like, you know, with with Ghosts and Goblins, there's really only like one or two ways

00:59:32.920 --> 00:59:34.100
you can handle. Yeah.

00:59:34.220 --> 00:59:39.580
Like going through a stage, whereas like with Gradius, it's like you can go up and then switch

00:59:39.580 --> 00:59:42.280
back down to down or you can go through the middle of the stage.

00:59:42.280 --> 00:59:44.200
You can shoot open and go through a cave.

00:59:44.200 --> 00:59:47.060
I think that's kind of cool that they they accounted for that.

00:59:47.760 --> 00:59:52.060
All right. So since we were talking about difficulty and stuff like that, I think I need to address

00:59:52.060 --> 00:59:57.060
something for Joe here that, you know, I wouldn't say that this should change your opinion on

00:59:57.060 --> 00:59:59.840
the game, but there are continues.

01:00:00.440 --> 01:00:01.680
There are continues.

01:00:01.960 --> 01:00:03.740
There are continues in the game.

01:00:04.240 --> 01:00:09.280
And this is the very first instance of the Konami code.

01:00:10.180 --> 01:00:15.260
Yes. This was something that I ended up using, but not abusing.

01:00:15.620 --> 01:00:16.880
Do you know the Konami code?

01:00:17.020 --> 01:00:18.040
I don't. You do.

01:00:18.320 --> 01:00:19.500
OK, you ready? I might.

01:00:19.640 --> 01:00:22.140
But wait, I want to mention because this will come into play in a minute.

01:00:22.140 --> 01:00:27.120
But before you just told me that I wrote something in my notebook before I heard that and I put

01:00:27.120 --> 01:00:29.360
my pen down and later on, you'll find out what that was.

01:00:29.440 --> 01:00:31.200
But I want people to know that I didn't change this.

01:00:31.380 --> 01:00:36.820
OK, you made a note to yourself, a note to myself that I will talk about in a moment.

01:00:36.980 --> 01:00:42.080
But go on. Right. So but before I get any further, I'm sorry, I jumped ahead.

01:00:42.680 --> 01:00:45.780
The Konami code is is different than the continues code.

01:00:46.280 --> 01:00:49.060
OK, yeah, because I didn't think I got continues when I used it.

01:00:49.220 --> 01:00:55.020
Yeah, yeah, yeah. The Konami code is up, up, down, down, left, right, left, right, B.A.

01:00:55.780 --> 01:00:56.860
Joe, still not ringing?

01:00:57.160 --> 01:00:58.040
That rings a bell. OK, yeah, yeah.

01:00:58.040 --> 01:00:59.960
Where's that from? Every Konami game.

01:01:00.120 --> 01:01:03.420
It's mostly famous from Contra, I think, is like the famous one.

01:01:03.420 --> 01:01:04.540
Yeah, I think that's probably where I've heard it.

01:01:04.540 --> 01:01:08.900
Every like NES Konami game and even games way beyond that.

01:01:09.080 --> 01:01:15.640
And some websites. But there's a there's a something, a PlayStation game that's up, up, up, down, down, left, right,

01:01:15.680 --> 01:01:16.900
left, right, left, right. Probably a Konami game.

01:01:18.180 --> 01:01:19.040
Square? I want.

01:01:19.100 --> 01:01:20.580
Yeah, Square. Well, that would make sense.

01:01:20.760 --> 01:01:21.800
It's like B.A. instead.

01:01:22.060 --> 01:01:26.160
There's no B on PlayStation. The Konami code is like, yeah, like like Sean said, it's referenced on websites.

01:01:26.320 --> 01:01:30.000
I think even like Reddit used to have a thing that you could do to get something like that.

01:01:30.300 --> 01:01:34.160
Oh, yeah, it would have a narwhal show up on the show up on the homepage.

01:01:34.940 --> 01:01:36.740
But that's the Konami code.

01:01:36.920 --> 01:01:41.600
They're actually just if you pause the game while you're playing it, it gives you one

01:01:41.600 --> 01:01:47.160
speed up missiles, two options and a barrier force field, you know, like it's pretty much

01:01:47.160 --> 01:01:49.440
just like a way for you to continue to progress on.

01:01:50.020 --> 01:01:54.900
Yeah, I remember just to explain that I'm not so bad that I can't even get past the third

01:01:54.900 --> 01:01:56.380
level with the Konami code.

01:01:56.380 --> 01:02:01.400
I had played through the game and then I took up my I took a break from it and I decided

01:02:01.400 --> 01:02:03.000
I would try it out with the Konami code.

01:02:03.200 --> 01:02:04.440
I wasn't just going to keep playing.

01:02:04.700 --> 01:02:09.260
No, I love that. They give you the Konami code, though, because here's the thing about the Konami code.

01:02:09.500 --> 01:02:14.840
It it's not so game breaking like Game Genie where all of a sudden you have like I could

01:02:14.840 --> 01:02:18.460
just program the game to give me 99 lives or I can have the games this way.

01:02:18.520 --> 01:02:23.140
I'm invincible. It's like this gives you like a realistic way to be like, you know, I'm really enjoying

01:02:23.140 --> 01:02:27.620
this game, but I'm dying all the time and I just want to get to like as far as I can

01:02:27.620 --> 01:02:28.600
or I want to get further.

01:02:28.940 --> 01:02:29.820
I want to beat the game.

01:02:29.980 --> 01:02:33.180
This gives you like a realistic way of doing that, saying like, hey, you might have lost

01:02:33.180 --> 01:02:37.300
all your stuff, but just put in the Konami code and you'll get one speed up missiles,

01:02:37.600 --> 01:02:38.580
two options and a barrier.

01:02:38.900 --> 01:02:41.660
Yeah, it's it's it's fair as far as cheats go.

01:02:42.500 --> 01:02:47.640
The continue code, on the other hand, though, is when you get to the game over screen, you

01:02:47.640 --> 01:02:49.600
go down up B.A.

01:02:49.760 --> 01:02:55.780
B.A. and start and then it will start you at the last stage you visited.

01:02:56.280 --> 01:02:58.500
Wow. Interesting. I didn't know there were two codes.

01:02:58.600 --> 01:02:59.880
Yeah, it's very interesting that there are two.

01:02:59.960 --> 01:03:01.300
Well, there's more codes than that, too.

01:03:01.480 --> 01:03:03.320
There's a lot of secrets in this game.

01:03:03.580 --> 01:03:06.300
And I just think like the continue one changes a lot.

01:03:06.440 --> 01:03:09.820
Yeah, that makes that makes a big difference in like how you're playing this.

01:03:10.040 --> 01:03:12.820
I'll be honest. I use the continue code to get all the way to stage six.

01:03:12.820 --> 01:03:17.600
Like, I'm not going to pretend like I'm amazing at this game, but even the continue code won't

01:03:17.600 --> 01:03:19.760
save me to get to get to stage seven.

01:03:19.900 --> 01:03:21.160
You know, I still wasn't able to get there.

01:03:21.240 --> 01:03:24.180
So I respect these cheat codes because they don't outright break the game.

01:03:24.460 --> 01:03:31.240
Is this the first example of a literal cheat code in a in a game or for us?

01:03:31.500 --> 01:03:32.740
Yeah, I feel like it is.

01:03:32.900 --> 01:03:36.480
I feel like for for right now, you know, December 1986.

01:03:37.220 --> 01:03:38.740
Yeah. Wow. That's interesting.

01:03:39.120 --> 01:03:45.620
And I mean, the game also has like warp zones, too, and stage bonuses and even a way to skip

01:03:45.620 --> 01:03:46.460
stages.

01:03:47.320 --> 01:03:48.640
That's not the warp zones.

01:03:48.800 --> 01:03:51.720
That's not the warp zones that you can actually skip the stages.

01:03:51.960 --> 01:03:53.260
Here's an interesting way to do it.

01:03:53.320 --> 01:03:55.280
I would have been nowhere near that inverted volcano.

01:03:55.800 --> 01:04:00.720
You have to defeat the bosses within five seconds of them appearing.

01:04:00.920 --> 01:04:01.760
How do you do that?

01:04:01.880 --> 01:04:02.740
Not sorry.

01:04:02.760 --> 01:04:06.400
So not the sub bosses like the volcano and stuff like that.

01:04:06.400 --> 01:04:09.080
I'm talking about that core Millennium Falcon like thing.

01:04:09.240 --> 01:04:13.040
If you can kill that thing within five seconds, which if you have, I guess, enough power ups,

01:04:13.080 --> 01:04:16.980
you can do it. It'll actually let you skip the next stage only up to stage five.

01:04:17.080 --> 01:04:19.320
Once you get to stage five, you can't skip on onwards.

01:04:19.480 --> 01:04:21.800
But I think it's like a really neat speed run thing.

01:04:21.940 --> 01:04:23.320
Yeah. You know, if you're going for that.

01:04:23.440 --> 01:04:24.800
But the stage bonuses are cool.

01:04:24.940 --> 01:04:30.200
I mean, you know, Sean, you mentioned going through the like the cave like thing in

01:04:30.200 --> 01:04:31.740
was that stage two you were talking about?

01:04:31.900 --> 01:04:34.100
That's in stage one. OK, stage one where you're shooting.

01:04:34.360 --> 01:04:35.260
I shoot the mountain, right?

01:04:35.440 --> 01:04:39.760
Yeah. And then in stage two, there's a couple of different like paths that you can take with

01:04:39.760 --> 01:04:42.800
the pink orbs or the Kirby life.

01:04:43.120 --> 01:04:44.600
Yes. Joe might say.

01:04:44.920 --> 01:04:47.160
And so that's like throughout the different levels.

01:04:47.360 --> 01:04:50.940
There's stuff like that. I just think that's like really neat that they included all these little secrets again.

01:04:50.940 --> 01:04:53.160
Like another thing that reminds me of Super Mario Brothers.

01:04:53.740 --> 01:04:54.960
I thought you were going to say Star Fox.

01:04:55.220 --> 01:04:57.800
Yeah. No, no, no. Star Fox has a lot of secrets, though, guys.

01:04:57.980 --> 01:05:00.000
I cannot plug Star Fox 64.

01:05:00.520 --> 01:05:02.140
Not not Star Fox or Super Nintendo.

01:05:02.340 --> 01:05:03.620
That game runs like 10 FPS.

01:05:03.620 --> 01:05:04.920
Yes. Not even joking.

01:05:04.920 --> 01:05:06.040
Actually, I think it's like 15.

01:05:06.360 --> 01:05:08.480
Maybe interesting about Gradius here.

01:05:08.620 --> 01:05:09.600
Straight off for a minute.

01:05:10.040 --> 01:05:13.520
Gradius was also converted for the Nintendo verse series.

01:05:13.980 --> 01:05:15.080
Oh, yeah.

01:05:15.080 --> 01:05:16.120
How do you verse that?

01:05:16.340 --> 01:05:17.460
Isn't that weird?

01:05:17.660 --> 01:05:20.020
Like, first off, it's not a Nintendo game.

01:05:20.140 --> 01:05:22.780
It's a Konami game. So what's it doing in the Nintendo verse series?

01:05:22.960 --> 01:05:27.900
And is it? I don't know if it was called like verse Gradius or whatever, but it's identical to the NES

01:05:27.900 --> 01:05:35.200
version, but it includes no cheat codes, but allows the player to continue indefinitely.

01:05:35.700 --> 01:05:38.040
Wow. Well, isn't that because it's an arcade game?

01:05:38.460 --> 01:05:44.460
Well, so, yeah, but what I meant is, is like, basically, you never have to go back to stage

01:05:44.460 --> 01:05:45.600
one. Does that make sense?

01:05:45.760 --> 01:05:47.000
Like, like... Oh, okay. Yeah.

01:05:47.340 --> 01:05:49.760
I would just think, like, give me your money and you can keep playing.

01:05:49.900 --> 01:05:50.740
It's exactly...

01:05:50.740 --> 01:05:51.540
Yes, that's true.

01:05:51.760 --> 01:05:55.940
You have to put in the quarter to keep going, but it's another way to, like, get to the end

01:05:55.940 --> 01:05:58.480
faster. You know, you don't have to, like, start all over.

01:05:58.760 --> 01:06:04.960
Pay to win. Yeah, this is an early example of Konami being like, well, you know, we do like money.

01:06:05.980 --> 01:06:09.620
Ha ha. And this is a...

01:06:09.620 --> 01:06:12.320
One more weird thought I have, and then I'll open the floor.

01:06:12.580 --> 01:06:16.760
But I was looking for, like, developer interviews and stuff like that, and of course, I'm going

01:06:16.760 --> 01:06:19.980
to find a ton of that stuff on Shmupilations, which I'm going to link in the show notes.

01:06:19.980 --> 01:06:22.700
This is an amazing resource for people who are into shmups.

01:06:23.360 --> 01:06:28.660
But they included this, like, as part of, like, an interview with the director, and they

01:06:28.660 --> 01:06:29.860
didn't, like...

01:06:29.860 --> 01:06:31.040
They didn't source it.

01:06:31.160 --> 01:06:32.380
Like, they didn't say where they got it.

01:06:32.400 --> 01:06:34.180
It was, like, unknown article from 1996.

01:06:34.780 --> 01:06:39.180
And I just don't even know if this is true, but here's the quote, and just take this for

01:06:39.180 --> 01:06:46.260
what it's worth. Our offices were in Osaka during the Gradius development, and after we released the first

01:06:46.260 --> 01:06:53.720
game, a young kid of about elementary school age brought us 200,000 yen and asked us to

01:06:53.720 --> 01:06:54.900
sell him Gradius.

01:06:54.900 --> 01:07:00.940
Since this was a large amount of money, even to us, we called his parents to confirm, and

01:07:00.940 --> 01:07:02.680
they asked us to sell it to him.

01:07:02.820 --> 01:07:03.800
So we did.

01:07:05.000 --> 01:07:08.520
That we had a fan like this made me extremely happy.

01:07:09.040 --> 01:07:11.360
Does some child own Gradius?

01:07:12.000 --> 01:07:13.840
Yeah, wait, sold to him, like, what the...

01:07:13.840 --> 01:07:15.160
Wait, sold to him rights? It's like...

01:07:15.160 --> 01:07:17.820
200,000 yen is a ton of money, guys.

01:07:17.820 --> 01:07:21.480
But wait, 200,000 yen is, like, not that much money.

01:07:21.480 --> 01:07:24.120
Right. It's... Okay, sorry, yeah, it's not that much money, but...

01:07:24.120 --> 01:07:25.580
It's not even that much money.

01:07:25.820 --> 01:07:26.640
It's like 20...

01:07:26.640 --> 01:07:27.760
Isn't it $20,000?

01:07:27.940 --> 01:07:28.820
Like, $2,000? Yeah.

01:07:28.820 --> 01:07:30.360
He's an elementary school kid.

01:07:30.860 --> 01:07:32.920
It was like... It's $1,800 today.

01:07:32.920 --> 01:07:36.100
It's probably more like $3,000 in 1985.

01:07:36.680 --> 01:07:38.100
What is he doing with this?

01:07:38.260 --> 01:07:39.640
Is this story real?

01:07:39.860 --> 01:07:41.100
It cannot be real.

01:07:41.300 --> 01:07:42.500
Like... They're still making...

01:07:42.500 --> 01:07:44.360
I mean, not... They're not still making Gradius games.

01:07:44.360 --> 01:07:45.460
Did they really take this money, too?

01:07:45.460 --> 01:07:47.300
Well, that kid's old enough to be developing games now.

01:07:47.460 --> 01:07:49.320
I guess so. They took the money from a kid?

01:07:49.320 --> 01:07:51.740
Like, where did he get $200,000 yen?

01:07:51.740 --> 01:07:53.200
Hey, he's throwing the point like his parents...

01:07:53.200 --> 01:07:56.400
They probably only would have sold it to a kid, so the parents were like, alright, send

01:07:57.140 --> 01:07:58.420
this kid in there to sell it.

01:07:58.480 --> 01:08:00.900
You would think he'd have, like, $5 on him.

01:08:01.100 --> 01:08:02.840
You know? Like, he was like, oh, here's $5.

01:08:03.180 --> 01:08:04.740
Can I buy Gradius?

01:08:04.880 --> 01:08:06.600
And they were like, haha, that kid's so cute.

01:08:06.780 --> 01:08:07.980
Like, called his parents.

01:08:08.060 --> 01:08:09.360
Do you mind if we take his $5?

01:08:09.760 --> 01:08:10.880
They're like, yeah, that's his allowance.

01:08:11.060 --> 01:08:12.500
Like, go ahead. He loves your game.

01:08:12.680 --> 01:08:14.500
And then they're like, oh, would you like to be in the game?

01:08:14.560 --> 01:08:16.880
And then they put his brain in the game.

01:08:17.460 --> 01:08:19.140
They took a scan of his brain.

01:08:19.360 --> 01:08:20.700
They took a cat scan of his brain.

01:08:20.920 --> 01:08:22.280
And they just took his money.

01:08:22.620 --> 01:08:25.180
Like, I think that that's weird, but it's probably not a true story.

01:08:25.320 --> 01:08:28.900
Maybe Shmupelations puts that on there because they just, like...

01:08:28.900 --> 01:08:30.020
Like, control people? Yeah, yeah.

01:08:30.060 --> 01:08:31.280
Like, maybe they want to fact-trick people.

01:08:31.380 --> 01:08:35.000
Like, see if they're reading the interviews and, like, including these obscure...

01:08:35.580 --> 01:08:36.500
I don't know, honestly.

01:08:36.520 --> 01:08:41.540
If you're from Shmupelations or if you've ever read this or heard this story, like, let me know.

01:08:41.540 --> 01:08:43.600
Like, please, I'd love to find out if I...

01:08:43.600 --> 01:08:50.320
Like, now a grown adult, I guess, but at the time a child owned Gradius and let Konami continue to make games for them.

01:08:50.440 --> 01:08:51.940
They're like, yeah, yeah, yeah, you guys can make Gradius, too.

01:08:52.000 --> 01:08:55.380
That's fine. Can you imagine if he, like, if he was actually just, like, a real, like, hard hitter?

01:08:55.460 --> 01:08:58.560
As soon as they, like, made the deal, he was, like, just super straight with them.

01:08:58.720 --> 01:08:59.820
Yeah, yeah, he was like, okay, here's the deal.

01:08:59.820 --> 01:09:02.560
Like, he pulls out, like, spreadsheets he's made.

01:09:02.600 --> 01:09:04.640
He's like, this is the levels for Gradius, too.

01:09:05.180 --> 01:09:07.220
The final boss will be a giant Moai.

01:09:09.860 --> 01:09:12.720
It is time for the Essential Games List.

01:09:15.660 --> 01:09:18.280
All right, it's time for the Essential Games List.

